# The UI/UX Master Book

*A plain-English guide to designing interfaces people understand — assembled from the design
library behind Kapruka's UI/UX/CRO agents.*

## What this book is

Three books went into this: *The Ultimate Handbook of UI/UX Product Design* (Lise Pilot), *UX and
UI Strategy* (Pamala Deacon), and *Roots of UI/UX Design* (Elisa Paduraru). Each covers similar
ground from a different angle — process, strategy, visual craft. This book merges them into one
narrative, cuts the repetition, and explains the *why* behind each rule in plain language, not just
the rule itself.

It's organized in two halves because the discipline splits in two:

- **UX (User Experience)** is the *blueprint*. It's invisible when done well — the research, the
  flow, the structure that decides what happens when someone taps a button. UX asks "does this
  work?" and "does this make sense?"
- **UI (User Interface)** is the *paint, furniture, and light fixtures*. It's what people actually
  see and touch — color, type, spacing, buttons. UI asks "does this look right?" and "does this
  feel good to use?"

UX comes first because you can't decorate a floor plan that doesn't exist yet. But neither one
works alone — a beautifully designed screen with a confusing flow fails just as hard as a
perfectly logical flow that's ugly and hard to read.

