## Chapter 8 — Designing for Everyone

Accessibility isn't a feature you bolt on at the end — it's one of the core things that makes a
design good or bad in the first place. Over a billion people live with some form of disability;
designing without them in mind isn't a neutral choice, it's an active exclusion.

**The plain-language version of what to do:**

- Make sure text is genuinely readable against its background (see the numbers in Part II —
  contrast isn't a matter of taste, it's measurable).
- Every interactive element should be fully usable with a keyboard alone — no mouse-only traps.
- Label everything — form fields, buttons, images — with real, descriptive text. "Click here" tells
  a screen-reader user nothing; "Submit your order" does.
- Never make color the *only* way information is conveyed — a colorblind user needs the same
  information from text, an icon, or a pattern too.
- Test with real assistive technology — screen readers like NVDA, JAWS, or VoiceOver — and ideally
  with real users who have disabilities, not just automated tools.
- Offer a dark mode where it makes sense; it genuinely reduces eye strain for a meaningful share of
  users.

This is a legal requirement in many places (ADA, Section 508, the EU Web Accessibility Directive)
— but treat it as a design requirement first, and the legal box checks itself.

---

## Chapter 9 — Designing for Thumbs

Over half of web traffic is mobile — design the smallest screen first, then expand outward, not
the other way around.

- **Streamline navigation.** A mobile screen has no room for the header nav that worked on desktop
  — decide what actually matters and cut the rest.
- **Design for thumbs, literally.** Keep primary actions within comfortable thumb reach; don't
  place critical controls in corners that require a hand shift to reach.
- **Declutter aggressively.** Complexity that was fine on a large screen becomes unusable on a
  small one — every element has to justify its presence.
- **Use bigger text and native controls.** Don't repurpose a desktop-style dropdown for mobile when
  the platform has a native picker that people already know how to use.
- **Minimize typing.** Every field you can remove, autofill, or replace with a tap is a field
  someone won't abandon your form over.
- **Keep the experience consistent** across web and mobile — someone switching devices mid-task
  shouldn't feel like they're using a different product.

