## Chapter 10 — Recurring Patterns Worth Getting Right

A few components show up in nearly every product. Getting these specifically right pays off
disproportionately, because they're where most of the friction actually lives:

- **Cards** — put the most critical content at the top; keep the copy concise; one clear link per
  card (multiple competing links inside a card just confuses people); keep the primary and
  secondary actions visually distinct; keep framing consistent across a set so a grid of cards
  doesn't look chaotic.
- **Forms** — only ask for what you genuinely need. Every extra field is a small tax on completion.
  Include a Terms consent checkbox wherever you're processing personal data.
- **Sign up / sign in** — clear field labels, real-time validation (an email that's obviously
  malformed, a password that's obviously too short), a Terms checkbox with a real link, a
  "Remember Me" option on sign-in, and always a visible link between the two screens so someone who
  landed on the wrong one isn't stuck.
- **Checkout / cart** — show line items, quantities, subtotal, tax, and shipping clearly; let
  people edit quantities in place; keep a "Continue Shopping" option; auto-fill addresses where
  possible; make "Confirm Payment" visually unmistakable from everything else on the page.
- **Loading states** — show progress on anything that isn't instant. Silence during a wait reads as
  a crash, not patience.
- **Empty and error states** — never strand someone on a blank or confusing screen. Acknowledge
  what happened and give a clear next step — "no results found, try broadening your search" beats a
  blank white page every time.
- **Tables** — left-align text, right-align numbers, center icons/badges. Generous line height.
  Paginate instead of showing everything at once, and add sortable headers and filters once the
  data set is big enough to need them.
- **FAQs** — generous whitespace, icons that clearly signal "this expands." A good FAQ genuinely
  reduces support load and helps search visibility as a side effect.

