## Chapter 12 — Guiding the Eye

Visual hierarchy is the art of making sure someone's eye lands on the most important thing first,
and then in the right order after that.

- **One clear focal point per screen.** Everything else is secondary or tertiary — if three things
  are all fighting to be "the most important," none of them win.
- **Size, weight, color, contrast, spacing, and position all encode importance.** Bigger, bolder,
  higher-contrast, and more isolated all read as "more important," whether or not that's what you
  meant.
- **Whitespace is a tool, not empty space you forgot to fill.** Isolating an element increases how
  important it feels, and space is what separates one group of related things from another.
- **Validate it.** Eye-tracking or heat-map tools (Hotjar, Tobii) confirm whether attention is
  actually landing where you intended.
- **The first click predicts success.** If the first thing someone taps doesn't move them toward
  their goal, the hierarchy — or the navigation — is wrong, no matter how good it looks.

