## Chapter 13 — Grids & Layout

A grid is just intersecting lines that create columns, rows, gutters, and margins — structure that
makes alignment and responsiveness possible instead of accidental.

- **Use a column count that divides evenly** — 12 is the default (splits cleanly into halves,
  thirds, and quarters), 6 or 8 also work. Avoid 7, 9, or 11 — they don't divide cleanly and make
  consistent layouts harder to build.
- **Gutters** are the space between columns; **margins** are the space around the whole grid.
- **Fluid grids** flex their column widths with the screen (margins/gutters stay fixed) — use this
  for anything responsive. **Fixed grids** stay a constant size regardless of screen — use this
  only when content genuinely shouldn't reflow.
- **Desktop:** 12-column grid, gutters around 12–16pt, margins around 160–180pt.
- **The 8-point rule:** size and space everything in multiples of 8 (8, 16, 24, 32, 40...). Most
  screens divide evenly by 8, so this keeps rhythm consistent and makes handoff to developers
  faster because there's no ambiguity about "was that supposed to be 15px or 16px?"
- **Mobile:** 8pt grid, margins around 16px (sometimes 20–24px), gutters 16px by default — don't go
  below 8px except in genuinely tight, minimal layouts.
- **Give the design room to breathe.** Ample space between text, images, and buttons isn't wasted —
  it's what makes the layout feel calm instead of crowded.

---

## Chapter 14 — Typography

- **Typeface vs. font:** the *typeface* is the overall family (Helvetica, Roboto); a *font* is one
  specific style within it (Regular, Bold, Italic). Choose a typeface for legibility, how well it
  scales, and how many weights it offers — a family with only one weight boxes you in later.
- **Categories:** Sans Serif reads clean, modern, informal. Serif reads classic and sophisticated.
  Script fonts should be avoided for UI or body text — they're genuinely harder to read at
  interface sizes, whatever mood they add.
- **Line height and size move in opposite directions.** Larger text needs a *shorter* line height
  relative to its size; smaller text needs a *taller* one. As a rule of thumb for small text, line
  height ≈ font size × 1.6 (so 16pt text gets roughly 26pt of line height).
- **Line length matters more than people expect.** Too short and the eye has to jump lines
  constantly, breaking flow; too long and it's easy to lose your place returning to the start of
  the next line. Aim for **45–75 characters per line** for body text.
- **Alignment:** left-align by default — most languages read left to right, and most people are
  right-handed, so this is simply the path of least resistance. Center-align only for short text
  like a headline. Justify cautiously, and watch the "rag" (the uneven right edge) — an ugly rag
  looks like a mistake even when the content is fine.
- **Keep it to 1–2 typefaces.** Use weight, size, and case to create hierarchy instead of reaching
  for a third family — more families rarely reads as "richer," it reads as "inconsistent."
- **Base body text should be at least 16px.** Going smaller genuinely hurts readability and
  accessibility — it's not a stylistic nuance, it's a measurable usability cost.
- **Define a type scale up front** — at least 10 text styles, generated from a base size using a
  consistent ratio (commonly 1.2–1.25, a "modular scale"). A typical resulting ramp looks like
  12·14·16·20·24·32·40·48px. Cap it at **3 font sizes per section** so a screen doesn't turn into a
  ransom note.

