## Chapter 16 — Depth: Shadows, Gradients & Elevation

**Shadows** communicate hierarchy, depth, and relative position — their absence makes an interface
feel flatter and less "real." Three options: no shadow (flush with the surface), drop shadow
(raised above it), and inner shadow (embedded into it — common on inputs and in the Neumorphism
style).

To make a shadow read as physically plausible: set its offset, blur, and opacity deliberately;
never put the shadow *above* the object (light doesn't come from below, so it reads as wrong even
if you can't say why); and combine a tight, darker "core shadow" right at the object's edge with a
wider, lighter "cast shadow" behind it, rather than one flat blur — real shadows have both. Shadow
size should scale with object size. In dark mode, shadows should never be pure white — use a darker
shade of the element's own color plus a lighter tint of the background instead.

**Gradients** are a gradual transition between two or more colors, used to add a sense of realism
or depth to type, buttons, cards, and headers. Keep transitions gentle, use three colors at most in
a small shape, and keep color stops symmetric. A primary color blended into its own tints/shades is
always a safe combination. A primary blended straight into its complementary color can produce an
ugly gray midpoint — if you want that combination, insert a bridging color from elsewhere on the
wheel first.

