## Chapter 17 — Spacing & Rhythm

Whitespace isn't leftover space — it actively groups related things together and separates
unrelated ones, purely through proximity. Treat it as a real design element, the same way you'd
treat a color or a typeface, and define it as reusable tokens rather than eyeballing it screen by
screen.

**A standard spacing scale**, built on the same 8px base unit as the grid: 4 · 8 · 16 · 24 · 32 ·
48 · 64. Use 4px only for small optical corrections. Smaller gaps mean "these things are related";
larger gaps mean "these are different groups" — that's the whole mechanism, and it works because
it's consistent.

---

## Chapter 18 — Building Blocks

### Buttons

A button has to *look* interactive before anyone will treat it as one — shape, size, color,
shadow, and label all contribute to that signal. Rounded corners are the norm because they're
easier on the eye and keep attention on the label rather than the corners. For sizing: text around
16px, roughly 12px of padding for a "base" height, and a simple width rule of thumb — about twice
the height. Keep a real size ladder (xs / s / base / l / xl) rather than one-off dimensions per
screen.

Minimum touch targets: Material recommends at least 36×36px, Apple at least 40×40px (with ~44×44px
as the comfortable mobile target). Establish a clear hierarchy — primary (the main action),
secondary, and tertiary (low-impact) — but keep border-radius, typography, and shadow style shared
across all three levels so they read as a family. If every option on a screen is genuinely equal in
importance, resist the urge to impose a fake hierarchy just for variety.

Every button needs its full set of states defined: default, hover, active/pressed, in-progress,
focus, and disabled.

### Forms & inputs (visually)

Rounded corners are the norm here too. Relevant icons inside a field genuinely improve
intuitiveness (a calendar icon next to a date field, for instance). Constrain input width when it
makes the form look organized rather than sprawling. Always give visible state feedback — an error
message when something's wrong, a success icon when it's right — and design a dropdown as an input
plus a chevron so it visually reads as "there's a list here" before anyone clicks it.

### Icons

Icons work because images are processed faster than text — so keep them simple and instantly
recognizable, not clever. Pick one style (outline, glyph, or duo-tone — duo-tone is the most common
and legible) and never mix styles within a product; a gradient icon next to a flat one reads as a
mistake even if both individually look fine. Contain icons in a fixed frame (16×16, 24×24, or
32×32px) so scaling stays predictable, keep the palette to 3–4 colors, and match line weight across
the whole set. When an icon sits next to text in a navbar or sidebar, don't match it exactly to the
text color — a tint at 50–70% opacity of the primary color keeps it from competing with the label
for attention. And never rely on an icon alone to carry critical meaning — pair it with a label,
both for clarity and for accessibility.

### Images

Brains process images dramatically faster than text, and most of what people absorb from a page is
visual — which is exactly why a bad image choice does real damage. Establish a clear focal point so
crops don't lose context across different screen sizes; if an image is hard to make sense of at a
small size, it's the wrong image, not a cropping problem. Compress before uploading — it's faster
*and* it looks sharper, since a blurry image reads as low quality regardless of the actual content.
When placing text over an image, apply a dark or brand-colored filter at 50–80% opacity so the text
stays legible without losing all the image's detail underneath. The rule of thirds — dividing the
frame into nine parts and placing the subject on a line or intersection — creates more visual
interest than dead-centering everything.

### Illustrations

Illustrations convey emotion and information fast, and can substitute for photography where a photo
would feel too literal or too corporate. Keep enough detail visible at whatever size you're using —
don't cram a detailed illustration into a small space, use a simplified version instead. Keep a
cohesive visual theme (consistent color tones, consistent character style) across a whole set.
Common places they earn their keep: hero sections, onboarding flows, empty states, and error pages
— exactly the moments where a screen would otherwise feel cold or broken.

