## Chapter 21 — Visual Styles You'll Recognize

Pick one direction and apply it consistently — mixing styles within one product is the single
fastest way to make it look unplanned.

- **Flat** — 2D, minimalist, no gradients or shadows or textures, bold vibrant colors, strict
  grid-based layout.
- **Material** (Google, 2014) — elevation communicated through shadows (a real z-axis hierarchy),
  a consistent icon set, bold color used to carry information, card-based layouts on a responsive
  grid.
- **Neubrutalism** — large, heavy sans-serif type, thick black borders on cards and buttons,
  high-contrast color, deliberately asymmetric or irregular grids that break the usual conventions
  on purpose.
- **Bauhaus** — geometric sans-serif type, primitive shapes (squares, triangles, circles), a
  limited palette (primary red/yellow/blue, or black-and-white plus one accent), strict grid
  structure.
- **Neumorphism** — elements appear to melt into the background, soft shadows both inward and
  outward, very low contrast and subtle color, a clean and uncluttered feel. (Worth noting: this
  style is genuinely difficult to make accessible because of its low contrast by design — use it
  carefully.)
- **Glassmorphism** — a frosted-glass transparency effect where the background shows through,
  paired with vibrant saturated backgrounds and minimalist, legible type. Common on blurred navbars,
  hero sections, and glass-style icons.

