## Chapter 22 — The Accessibility Numbers

Everything in Chapter 8 was the *why*. Here's the *exactly how much* — the concrete, testable
numbers that turn "make it accessible" from an opinion into something you can check with a tool.

| What | The number |
|---|---|
| Normal text contrast | at least **4.5:1** against its background |
| Large text contrast (≥24px, or ≥18.66px bold) | at least **3:1** |
| AAA-level contrast (stricter standard) | 7:1 normal, 4.5:1 large |
| UI components & meaningful graphics | at least **3:1** contrast |
| Base body font size | at least **16px** |
| Body line-height | **1.4–1.6** (tighter, 1.1–1.25, for large headings) |
| Line length | **45–75 characters** |
| Text resize support | up to **200%** without losing content or function |
| Reflow width | content should reflow cleanly at **320px** |
| Touch target size | **≥44×44px** (Apple) / **48×48dp** (Material), with ~8px between targets |
| Base spacing unit | **8px** (4px for fine adjustments) |

Four principles sit behind every one of these numbers — they spell **POUR**:

- **Perceivable** — content and interface elements must be presentable in ways everyone can
  perceive (text alternatives for non-text content, adaptable presentation).
- **Operable** — interface components must be operable by everyone (full keyboard access, enough
  time to act, nothing that triggers seizures).
- **Understandable** — content and operation must be understandable (readable text, predictable
  navigation, help recovering from errors).
- **Robust** — content must work reliably across current and future assistive technologies.

Use semantic HTML (`<header>`, `<nav>`, `<button>`) as the foundation, and only add ARIA roles or
labels where plain HTML genuinely falls short. Test with a real contrast checker (WebAIM's is free
and standard), run an automated audit (axe, WAVE, or Lighthouse), and do at least one real pass with
a screen reader — automated tools catch maybe half of what actually matters.

